NAME
       systemd.timer - timer units: start a service on a schedule

SYNOPSIS
       NAME.timer

DESCRIPTION
       A timer unit starts another unit, normally the service with the same
       name (backup.timer starts backup.service), at set times or after set
       delays. Timers are systemd's alternative to cron: their runs appear
       in the journal, they can catch up on runs missed while the machine was
       off, and they can be listed with systemctl list-timers.

       Enable and start the TIMER, not the service: systemctl enable --now
       backup.timer. The service needs no [Install] section of its own; it is
       usually Type=oneshot.

       Besides [Unit] and [Install] (with WantedBy=timers.target), a timer
       has a [Timer] section.

THE [Timer] SECTION
       OnCalendar=WHEN
              Run at calendar times. The format is
              DAY-OF-WEEK YEAR-MONTH-DAY HOUR:MINUTE:SECOND, where any part
              may be * and the day of week and the date may be left out:
                  *-*-* 02:00:00       every day at 02:00
                  Mon..Fri 08:30       weekdays at 08:30
                  *-*-01 00:00:00      the first of every month
                  *:0/15               every 15 minutes
              The words hourly, daily, weekly, monthly and yearly work too.
              Several OnCalendar lines are allowed. After enabling, check
              the next run time with systemctl list-timers.

       OnBootSec=TIME
              Run this long after the machine booted (such as 15min).

       OnUnitActiveSec=TIME
              Run this long after the unit last started (repeating).

       OnActiveSec=TIME
              Run this long after the timer itself was started.

       Persistent=true
              With OnCalendar: if the machine was off at the planned time,
              run as soon as possible after the next boot.

       AccuracySec=TIME
              How exact the time must be (1min by default; systemd may run
              the job up to this much later to group wake-ups).

       RandomizedDelaySec=TIME
              Add a random delay up to TIME.

       Unit=NAME
              Start this unit instead of the service with the same name.

       Times may be written as 30s, 5min, 2h, 1d, or combined (1h 30min).

EXAMPLES
       Run /usr/local/bin/backup.sh every day at 02:00 with a timer:

           cat > /etc/systemd/system/backup.service <<'EOF'
           [Unit]
           Description=Nightly backup
           [Service]
           Type=oneshot
           ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/backup.sh
           EOF

           cat > /etc/systemd/system/backup.timer <<'EOF'
           [Unit]
           Description=Run the nightly backup
           [Timer]
           OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:00:00
           Persistent=true
           [Install]
           WantedBy=timers.target
           EOF

           systemctl daemon-reload
           systemctl enable --now backup.timer
           systemctl list-timers backup.timer

       Change how often a packaged timer runs, with a drop-in (the empty
       OnUnitActiveSec= clears the old schedule first):

           systemctl edit systemd-tmpfiles-clean.timer
               [Timer]
               OnUnitActiveSec=
               OnUnitActiveSec=12h

